{
  "result": "At dawn, the city is least interested in being a city. Delivery trucks murmur along empty streets, bakery windows glow like small hearths, and pigeons conduct their patient parliament on telephone wires. The day has not yet chosen its mood. It might become impatient, bright, generous, or strange. For a few minutes, however, everything seems willing to wait.\n\nA woman waters three plants outside a laundromat, though rain is predicted. A man in a blue coat unlocks a bookstore and pauses to read a note taped to the glass. Somewhere, a radio begins playing an old song with too much optimism. These small actions do not announce themselves. They simply happen, and that may be their grace.\n\nBy noon, the streets will be full of errands, arguments, appointments, and bright screens asking for attention. The morning will disappear beneath schedules. Still, perhaps some fragment of it remains: the bakery light, the watered leaves, the unhurried pigeons.\n\nI like to think that quiet moments are not empty spaces between important events. They are events themselves, brief rooms in which the world remembers it does not need to perform. Anyone who notices them carries away a private kind of wealth: a little more patience, a little more wonder, and a reason to keep walking slowly, even when the traffic begins and the day insists on hurrying.\n\nTomorrow, another dawn will arrive without applause. The city will loosen its shoulders, open its eyes, and offer the same quiet invitation: look closely, linger, begin.",
